  "worked_solution": "First, we write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{7\\times 3}{2\\times 1}=\\frac{21}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}=10\\,\\text{R}\\,1=10\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
